From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 11:23:52 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] iio: qcom_smgr: clear samples and give them a timestamp

Every IIO device of the driver has a timestamp channel, but the report
handler pushed a sample from an uninitialised stack variable with
iio_push_to_buffers() and never set its timestamp. With the timestamp
enabled (iio-sensor-proxy enables every scan element), each accelerometer,
gyroscope and magnetometer sample carried eight bytes of kernel stack to
userspace in place of the timestamp, and the four bytes of padding before
it. The single-channel devices (light, proximity, pressure) have their
timestamp at offset 8, where userspace got the third sample word and the
padding instead.

Clear the sample, push it with iio_push_to_buffers_with_ts(), which puts
the time of reception where the active scan layout wants it, and align
the timestamp member as IIO requires.

The driver is in the msm8996-mainline tree (v6.19.5-msm8996), not in
mainline yet.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820). Before, the four bytes before the
timestamp and the timestamp itself held a kernel virtual address
(0xffff8000...) in every accelerometer sample; after, the padding is
zero and the timestamps follow CLOCK_REALTIME 2.5 ms apart at 400 Hz.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 8 ++++++--
 include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h     | 2 +-
 2 files changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index 253e150903ab..b4f016bdab50 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -287,8 +287,11 @@ static void qcom_smgr_buffering_report_handler(struct qmi_handle *hdl,
 
 	/*
 	 * Construct data to be passed to IIO. Since we are matching report rate
-	 * with sample rate, we only get a single sample in every report.
+	 * with sample rate, we only get a single sample in every report. Clear
+	 * it first, so that the padding before the timestamp does not pass
+	 * stack contents to userspace.
 	 */
+	memset(&iio_data, 0, sizeof(iio_data));
 	for (j = 0; j < ARRAY_SIZE(ind->samples[0].values); ++j)
 		iio_data.values[j] = ind->samples[0].values[j];
 
@@ -315,7 +318,8 @@ static void qcom_smgr_buffering_report_handler(struct qmi_handle *hdl,
 		iio_data.values[0] = cpu_to_le32(temp);
 	}
 
-	iio_push_to_buffers(sensor->iio_dev, &iio_data);
+	iio_push_to_buffers_with_ts(sensor->iio_dev, &iio_data, sizeof(iio_data),
+				    iio_get_time_ns(sensor->iio_dev));
 }
 
 static const struct qmi_msg_handler qcom_smgr_msg_handlers[] = {
diff --git a/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h b/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
index 0dad50846e7a..25ee29a323c8 100644
--- a/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
+++ b/include/linux/iio/common/qcom_smgr.h
@@ -67,7 +67,7 @@ struct qcom_smgr_iio_priv {
 
 struct qcom_smgr_iio_data {
 	u32 values[3];
-	u64 timestamp;
+	aligned_s64 timestamp;
 };
 
 struct qcom_smgr {
-- 
